{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BiYvHDDZWjSiRUZJ3ceenn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260516T073712\n3\ttier: worst\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-16T08:33:40.558397+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: \"Classification and Generation Are Complementary Formulations for Code Execution Verification: An Empirical Characterization\"\n11\t\n12\t**Review mode**: full (2 Reviewers → roundtable convergence)\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Reviewer Assignments\n17\t\n18\t- **R1** — Methodology & Experimental Rigor\n19\t- **R2** — Novelty, Significance, Related Work & Presentation\n20\t\n21\t---\n22\t\n23\t## R1 Review (Method + Experiments)\n24\t\n25\t### Summary\n26\tThe paper compares per-test binary classification (CLS) and execution output generation (CWM) as formulations for execution-free code patch verification. It reports CLS matches or exceeds CWM accuracy with 1.9–8.8× GPU latency reduction, documents error complementarity (oracle ensemble 90.63–93.1%), and provides an oracle characterization of when per-test granularity adds value. Three model architectures are studied at function-level and repo-level granularity.\n27\t\n28\t### Strengths\n29\t- **Well-motivated research question.** The CLS-vs-CWM comparison for code verification is genuinely understudied.\n30\t- **Controlled function-level experiments.** Matched LoRA configurations, shared training data, and multi-seed evaluation provide a reasonable basis for function-level conclusions.\n31\t- **Transparent reporting of negatives.** The paper honestly reports the non-significant re-repair result (+0.5pp, p=0.72), the CWM token budget issue, and the architecture-dependent gap. This is commendable.\n32\t- **Multi-seed awareness.** The paper reports 3-seed means for repo-level CLS (Table 7) and acknowledges seed 42 is the best of three, avoiding the worst form of cherry-picking.\n33\t- **Error complementarity analysis is well-structured.** The asymmetry in error types (CLS: 75.5% FN, CWM: 83.1% FP) is a clean finding.\n34\t\n35\t### Weaknesses\n36\t\n37\t**W1. Repo-level results carry high seed variance (Critical).**\n38\tTable 7 reports repo-level CLS mean 79.88%±2.32 across 3 seeds, with seed 42 at 82.35% and seed 123 at 77.80%. The Fail F1 std is 3.87pp. This level of instability undermines precise gap claims. The paper appropriately reports the 3-seed mean, but the headline repo-level comparisons (Table 5) still use single-seed results per architecture (DeepSeek: 78.15%, Qwen-8B: 83.52%). Multi-seed validation for DeepSeek and 8B at repo level is absent.\n39\t\n40\t**W2. CWM repo-level comparison has a structural fairness issue (Major).**\n41\tThe initial CWM token budget of 64 tokens produced 93% unparseable outputs — the paper itself frames this as the starting point of a token budget sweep (Table 8). Even at 2048 tokens, CWM reaches only 75.5% with 18% still unparseable. The paper correctly frames this as \"characterizing the tradeoff\" rather than a ceiling, but the comparison conflates formulation difficulty with resource allocation. CLS gets the entire sequence budget for input context; CWM must split budget between input and generation. No experiment tests CWM with input context parity (e.g., longer total sequence length).\n42\t\n43\t**W3. DeepSeek multi-seed validation is insufficient (Moderate).**\n44\tOnly 2 seeds for DeepSeek-CWM (Table 4: 88.05%±0.23). The paper appropriately labels this \"preliminary,\" but then uses DeepSeek results to support the architecture-dependence claim (gap varies 0.52–2.43pp). Two seeds cannot distinguish real architecture effects from noise.\n45\t\n46\t**W4. Re-repair experiment is underpowered (Moderate).**\n47\tThe re-repair experiment (Tables 12-13) uses 200 instances. With 71.2% being T=1 (zero expected gain), only ~58 T≥2 instances are informative. The +2.5pp gain at T=2 (N=40) is interesting but not tested for significance. The experiment has insufficient power to detect the small effects the paper theorizes about.\n48\t\n49\t**W5. No CWM training quality verification across architectures (Moderate).**\n50\tDeepSeek-Coder drops 10.58pp from function to repo level (vs 5.88–6.08pp for Qwen). This could reflect genuine architecture differences or could indicate that the fixed LoRA hyperparameters (rank=16, α=32) are suboptimal for DeepSeek at repo scale. No hyperparameter sensitivity analysis is provided for the cross-architecture comparison.\n51\t\n52\t**W6. Error complementarity lacks multi-seed variance estimates (Minor).**\n53\tOracle ensemble of 90.63% (4B seed42) is a single-seed point estimate. The 5-seed mean (90.25%) is provided for 4B, but the 8B oracle (93.1%) appears to be from fewer seeds. The ±3pp stability claim for error type asymmetry is stated but not formally tested.\n54\t\n55\t### Questions for Authors\n56\t1. Can you provide multi-seed repo-level results for DeepSeek and Qwen-8B, not just Qwen-4B?\n57\t2. Have you tested CWM with longer total sequence length (e.g., 8192 with 2048 for generation) to achieve input context parity with CLS?\n58\t3. What is the statistical significance of the +2.5pp T=2 re-repair gain (Table 13)?\n59\t4. Did you conduct any hyperparameter search for CWM training, or were CLS hyperparameters transferred directly?\n60\t\n61\t### R1 Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)\n62\t\n63\t---\n64\t\n65\t## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n66\t\n67\t### Summary\n68\tThe paper presents an empirical characterization comparing classification and generation formulations for code execution verification. It introduces SWE-bench-PerTest (458 instances, per-test labels), reports architecture-dependent accuracy gaps, efficiency advantages for CLS, and systematic error complementarity between formulations. The paper positions itself as a characterization study rather than proposing a new method.\n69\t\n70\t### Strengths\n71\t- **Practical relevance.** The verification bottleneck in automated repair pipelines is real, and the formulation comparison is timely.\n72\t- **Clean experimental design at function level.** The matched-condition comparison isolates formulation effects reasonably well.\n73\t- **Useful dataset contribution.** SWE-bench-PerTest (458 instances × 16 candidates = 115,872 pairs) with per-test labels fills a gap in available benchmarks.\n74\t- **Calibrated claims.** The paper's pivot from \"CLS dominates\" to \"CLS and CWM are complementary\" is more defensible and more interesting. The architecture-dependence finding (DeepSeek gap 0.52pp vs Qwen-8B gap 2.43pp) adds nuance.\n75\t- **Honest negative results.** The re-repair non-result and the CWM token budget caveat are reported transparently.\n76\t\n77\t### Weaknesses\n78\t\n79\t**W1. Limited novelty of the core finding (Major).**\n80\tThe central observation — that predicting a single binary token is more reliable than generating variable-length output strings, and that the advantage grows with output complexity — is intuitive. The three CWM failure modes (truncation, string mismatch, format compliance) are well-known generation challenges. The paper characterizes them carefully but does not offer a surprising insight or a method that exploits the characterization. The error complementarity finding is more interesting but remains at the oracle level — no practical ensemble is demonstrated.\n81\t\n82\t**W2. Paper reads as a preliminary study, not a complete contribution (Major).**\n83\tThe paper identifies a phenomenon (CLS-CWM complementarity), characterizes it across conditions, but stops short of actionable outcomes. The re-repair experiment is negative. The ensemble is oracle-only. No downstream integration with a repair pipeline is shown. For a top venue (~25% acceptance), reviewers expect either a surprising finding or a method that advances the state of the art. This paper offers a careful but unsurprising characterization.\n84\t\n85\t**W3. Sparse and poorly formatted references (Major for submission quality).**\n86\tOnly 6 references in the bibliography. Several entries use \"and 1 others\" format, which is a .bib formatting error (using `and others` instead of full author lists). Missing important references:\n87\t- Process/outcome reward models (Lightman et al. 2023, Wang et al. 2024)\n88\t- Test-case-aware code ranking (AlphaCode, Li et al. 2022)\n89\t- Constrained decoding / output-space reduction literature\n90\t- The paper's function-level setup appears CRUXEval-inspired but citation coverage of this lineage is thin.\n91\t\n92\t**W4. Limited model diversity undermines generality (Major).**\n93\tThree architectures (Qwen3-4B, Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B) from two model families. The non-monotonic gap trend (Qwen-4B: 1.25pp, DeepSeek: 0.52pp, Qwen-8B: 2.43pp) could reflect architecture-specific quirks rather than fundamental formulation properties. The paper acknowledges this but still makes broad formulation-level claims in the title and abstract.\n94\t\n95\t**W5. Presentation issues (Minor).**\n96\t- Abstract is dense with numbers — could be more readable with fewer specifics\n97\t- The decision framework (Section 5.6 bullets) is useful but would benefit from a figure or decision tree\n98\t- No standalone Discussion section header — discussion is distributed across Section 6 and the Conclusion\n99\t- Repetition of key numbers (e.g., oracle 90.63%) across abstract, intro, results, and conclusion\n100\t\n101\t### Questions for Authors\n102\t1. What is the intended contribution type — empirical analysis paper, dataset paper, or methods paper? The current framing tries to be all three but succeeds most as analysis.\n103\t2. Have you attempted any non-oracle ensemble (e.g., learned routing, confidence-based switching)? Even a simple heuristic would strengthen the practical significance.\n104\t3. Why are there only 6 references? For a paper spanning code verification, execution simulation, program repair, and LLM evaluation, this is far below the typical citation count. Are there venue-specific constraints?\n105\t4. The error complementarity is the most robust finding — could this be the paper's sole focus, with deeper analysis (per-error-type breakdown, difficulty stratification, architecture interaction effects)?\n106\t\n107\t### R2 Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## Roundtable Convergence\n112\t\n113\t### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n114\tR1 and R2 reviewed each other's assessments. Key observations:\n115\t- R1's statistical concerns (seed variance, power analysis) complement R2's novelty/significance concerns\n116\t- Both independently flagged repo-level underpowered results as the single biggest issue\n117\t- Both independently scored 3/Weak Reject with confidence 4\n118\t\n119\t### Step 3: Areas of Agreement and Disagreement\n120\t\n121\t**Strong agreement (no disagreement):**\n122\t1. The function-level experiments are adequately controlled and provide useful characterization\n123\t2. Repo-level results are underpowered (single-seed per architecture except Qwen-4B) and carry the paper's most dramatic claims\n124\t3. CWM comparison fairness is compromised by token budget / input context asymmetry\n125\t4. Limited model diversity (effectively 2 families) is insufficient for the generality of claims made\n126\t5. The negative re-repair result (+0.5pp, p=0.72) is honestly reported but limits the paper's practical significance\n127\t6. Error complementarity is the most robust and interesting finding\n128\t7. The paper reads as a careful but incomplete study\n129\t8. References are sparse and contain formatting errors\n130\t\n131\t**Minor differences:**\n132\t- R1 weighs the CWM training quality concern more heavily (possible hyperparameter transfer failure)\n133\t- R2 weighs the novelty concern more heavily (the core finding is somewhat predictable)\n134\t- Both agree these are complementary rather than contradictory concerns\n135\t\n136\t### Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n137\t\n138\t**Overall Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n139\t\n140\t**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n141\t\n142\t**Consensus Justification:**\n143\t\n144\tThe paper addresses a relevant and understudied question — whether classification or generation is the better formulation for execution-free code verification — and arrives at the nuanced answer that they are complementary. The experimental design at function level is sound, the error complementarity analysis is the strongest contribution, and the transparency about negative results and limitations is commendable.\n145\t\n146\tHowever, several issues prevent acceptance at a top venue:\n147\t\n148\t1. **Underpowered repo-level experiments.** The most impactful claims (architecture-dependent gaps, efficiency scaling) rest on single-seed results per architecture at repo level. The documented 4.55pp range across 3 seeds for Qwen-4B (Table 7) indicates that single-seed comparisons are unreliable. DeepSeek and Qwen-8B lack multi-seed repo-level validation entirely.\n149\t\n150\t2. **CWM comparison fairness.** The token budget issue (93% unparseable at 64 tokens) and input context asymmetry between CLS and CWM mean the repo-level comparison conflates formulation difficulty with resource allocation. The paper acknowledges this but still draws formulation-level conclusions.\n151\t\n152\t3. **Limited novelty.** The core finding — binary classification is more reliable than variable-length generation, especially for complex outputs — is intuitive. The paper characterizes it well but does not surprise or offer a method that advances the state of the art. The most interesting finding (error complementarity) remains at the oracle level with no practical ensemble demonstrated.\n153\t\n154\t4. **Incomplete contribution arc.** The re-repair experiment is negative. The ensemble is oracle-only. The decision framework is theoretical. The paper characterizes a phenomenon but does not translate it into practical advances for code verification pipelines.\n155\t\n156\t5. **Sparse references with formatting errors.** Six references is far below standard for a paper spanning this many research areas. The \"and 1 others\" formatting errors in multiple entries signal incomplete preparation.\n157\t\n158\t**What would strengthen the paper:**\n159\t- Multi-seed repo-level results for all architectures (minimum 3 seeds each)\n160\t- CWM with input context parity (longer total sequence budget)\n161\t- At least one additional model family (CodeLlama or StarCoder)\n162\t- A working non-oracle ensemble demonstrating practical value\n163\t- Complete reference list with proper formatting\n164\t- Sharper focus on error complementarity as the primary contribution, with the characterization study supporting it\n165\t\n166\t**Suitability:** The paper is below the acceptance threshold for a top ML venue main conference. It could be suitable for a Findings track or workshop if the repo-level experiments are strengthened with multi-seed validation and the framing is tightened around the complementarity finding."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhGdAB7rLhJYE9F5kMPZRc","session_id":"9d47b78a-b580-467b-a50b-701a5443a345","uuid":"6f73dd54-8acc-48f3-937d-ae99961f3c89","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:26:07.304Z"}
