{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhGdAB7rLhJYE9F5kMPZRc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now let me compile the final report with all the information found:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've found and read all the key files you requested. Here's a comprehensive report:\n\n### 1. **experiment_plan.yaml** \n**Location**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/experiment_plan.yaml`\n\nThis file defines 13 experiments for the exec-sim-repair-verifier project, organized by priority (P0, P1, P2). Key experiments include:\n- **cwm-baseline**: Train CWM generation baseline (Qwen3-4B LoRA) for execution output prediction\n- **multi-seed-core**: Multi-seed validation with seeds {0, 1, 42}\n- **trace-then-judge**: Chain-of-thought ablation \n- **repo-level-training**: Fine-tune models on SWE-bench-PerTest data (Claims 1-2)\n- **llm-judge-baseline**: Zero-shot LLM-as-judge baselines\n- **swebench-pertest-data**: Docker re-evaluation to extract per-test labels\n\n### 2. **deepseek-cls-repo and deepseek-cwm-repo experiments (Status: RUNNING)**\n**Location**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/checkpoints/`\n\nFrom the checkpoint files, the DeepSeek experiments have status RUNNING:\n- **deepseek-cwm-repo**: DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B LoRA for repo-level code world model training (seed 42)\n  - Command uses swebench_full data\n  - max_seq_length=2048, batch_size=2, grad_accumulation=8\n  - Output dir: `/root/autodl-tmp/checkpoints_deepseek_cwm_repo/`\n  - Status: RUNNING as of 2026-05-16T08:05:24\n\n- **deepseek-cls-repo**: DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B for repo-level classification (seq8192)\n  - Output dir: `/root/autodl-tmp/checkpoints_deepseek_cls_repo/`\n  - Restarted with seq8192 configuration\n  - Status: Previously RUNNING\n\n### 3. **\"Worst\" Tier Scenario Definition**\n**Location**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T073712/meta.yaml` (lines 75-109)\n\nThe \"worst\" tier represents a pessimistic outcome scenario with key assumptions:\n- **Repo-level CLS accuracy <80%** or seed variance >5pp (undermines stability claims)\n- **Function-level 2.43pp gap** deemed not practically significant by reviewers\n- **DeepSeek experiments** fail or show different patterns, weakening architecture-independence claims\n- **No downstream re-repair experiments**, making per-test diagnostic value purely theoretical\n\n**Narrative Direction for Worst Case**: Pivot to three complementary angles:\n1. **Efficiency equivalence** — CLS achieves same accuracy as CWM with 1-token output (10-50x inference cost reduction)\n2. **Oracle characterization** — Decision framework for when per-test helps (T≥2, regression rate ≥10%, block-correlated errors)\n3. **Error complementarity** — CLS/CWM have systematic opposite failure modes (CLS: 75.5% false negatives; CWM: 83.1% false positives), oracle ensemble 90.63%\n\nThis scenario shifts framing from \"classification is better\" to \"classification and generation are complementary formulations with predictable failure modes.\"\n\n### 4. **paper_framing.md** (Main version)\n**Location**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/paper_framing.md`\n\nDefines three core claims for EMNLP 2026 (ARR May 25, 2026 deadline):\n\n1. **Complexity-Dependent Reformulation Advantage**: Classification ≈ CWM on function-level (~87% F1), but CLS scales better at 8B (gap 2.43pp) and dominates repo-level where CWM fails (token exhaustion, string prediction errors)\n\n2. **Diagnostic Granularity At Equivalent Accuracy**: Per-test classification achieves equivalent accuracy to trajectory-level (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin) while providing actionable test-level diagnostics\n\n3. **Accuracy-Utility Frontier Characterization**: Per-test advantage concentrates in instances with T≥2 FAIL_TO_PASS tests (31% of SWE-bench Verified); P2P regression provides signal only when rate >10%; block-correlated errors amplify advantage\n\n### 5. **paper_framing.md** (Worst Tier Version)\n**Location**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T073712/worst/paper_framing.md`\n\nIdentical core content but with modified narrative direction. The worst-tier paper reframes from claiming CLS superiority to characterizing complementarity, focusing on: (1) efficiency parity, (2) oracle-level decision framework, (3) error type complementarity as a verification diversity strategy.\n\n### 6. **Review Report for Worst Scenario**\n**Location**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T073712/worst/review_report.md`\n\nDetailed review showing Weak Reject (score 3) with two reviewers' consensus on:\n- **Critical weakness**: Repo-level results underpowered (4.55pp seed variance documented, single-seed comparisons unreliable)\n- **Major issues**: CWM comparison fairness (token budget asymmetry), limited novelty (binary classification > variable-length output is intuitive), incomplete contribution (negative re-repair result, oracle-only ensemble)\n- **Key finding**: Error complementarity is the most robust contribution (75.5% FN vs 83.1% FP asymmetry)\n- **Sparse references**: Only 6 citations with formatting errors\n- **Positive aspects**: Well-motivated, controlled function-level experiments, transparent negative reporting"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d47b78a-b580-467b-a50b-701a5443a345","uuid":"59cdcad8-f69e-42ee-9c95-1d5922b11c46","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:26:34.119Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16, find and read:\n1. The experiment_plan YAML or MD file (look for experiment_plan.yaml, experiment_plan.md, or similar)\n2. Any 